Well, we reached out to director Jennifer Salata, and she sent us in some great tidbits.

265
00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.260
We're going to share them throughout.

266
00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.520
You guys all know that have been listening.

267
00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:44.720
Jen is part of our office family for many years, and it was so great to hear her voice.

268
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:45.140
Yes.

269
00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:48.760
We're going to start with what I'm calling a fakie conference room meeting.

270
00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.180
So Marv and his team, they're there.

271
00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:54.180
They're in the big bullpen, and they're watching.

272
00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:58.700
And Ned is in the conference room with his crew, and he's like, okay, he's watching us.

273
00:10:58.820 --> 00:10:59.360
He's watching us.

274
00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.880
Like, jump up and down, high five, clap.

275
00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:02.660
Oh, my gosh.

276
00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:03.100
Point.

277
00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:03.480
Yes.

278
00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:03.880
Woo-hoo.

279
00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:09.840
And they're just acting like they're having this really great, awesome brainstorming meeting

280
00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:11.240
for ideas for the paper.

281
00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.660
And Nicole says, why don't we just have a real meeting?

282
00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.400
Adam thinks it's the most fun meeting they've ever had.

283
00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:17.840
Yeah.

284
00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.060
But it worked.

285
00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.340
Marv is watching them from across the room.

286
00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:22.400
I goes, wow, impressive.

287
00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.040
You know what this reminded me of?

288
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:24.820
What?

289
00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.320
The scene from Boys and Girls when Jan and all the ladies are in the conference room,

290
00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.120
and Michael literally gathers all the men in the bullpen.

291
00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:34.280
And he's like, okay, now everybody clap.

292
00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:35.080
Everybody clap.

293
00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:36.220
Oh, laughing, laughing.

294
00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.980
The difference is that Jan was not buying it at all.

295
00:11:39.260 --> 00:11:39.420
No.

296
00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.300
Marv was convinced, Jan, not so much.

297
00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:43.000
Not so much.

298
00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.760
Well, now Ned is going to leave the conference room.

299
00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:46.860
He's going to welcome Marv.

300
00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:48.200
He's like, oh, I didn't even know you were there.

301
00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:51.620
And he kind of sucks up to him a little bit.

302
00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:52.380
He does.

303
00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:57.560
And I want to shout out Alan Havy, who plays Marv.

304
00:11:57.680 --> 00:11:59.480
He's excellent in this episode.

305
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.380
Jen actually sent us in an audio clip about working with him.

306
00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:04.760
So I think we should hear it.

307
00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:12.680
I directed episode 107 of the paper, and I got to work with Alan Havy, who played Marv,

308
00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:13.600
who was Ned's boss.

309
00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:14.880
And he was brilliant.

310
00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:16.600
And I loved working with him.

311
00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.280
And I thought that I just recognized him from TV, maybe from Mad Men or from movies that

312
00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:21.620
he's done.

313
00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:27.140
And it wasn't until much later, like embarrassingly later, that I realized, oh my God, I directed

314
00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:33.260
him on The Office, on the episode called Promos, ninth season of The Office.

315
00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:38.840
He played Esther's dad, and he was trying to like swindle Dwight in some tractor deal.

316
00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.600
And he was also fantastic in that.

317
00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:43.040
Two very different roles.

318
00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:46.540
But I did not realize that I had worked with him before.

319
00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:51.120
And he was just such a delight in the character of Marv and dealing with Ned and his daddy issues.

320
00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:56.660
You know, it's so funny, Angela, the first time I saw him on the paper, I had forgotten

321
00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.600
that he played Esther's dad as well.

322
00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:00.500
I just remembered him from Mad Men.

323
00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:03.420
But he's so different.

324
00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:04.480
He's so different.

325
00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:07.000
And we both had that moment of like, oh, crap.

326
00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:08.500
That was, oh my God.

327
00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:12.580
But, you know, in that episode, he has like a baseball hat on.

328
00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:13.700
He's a little unshaven.

329
00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:15.200
He totally transformed.

330
00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:15.820
Yes.

331
00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.940
But what a great actor.

332
00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.980
Listen, we're going to get to it about Ned's daddy.

333
00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:26.040
But there is something about Alan and the way he portrays Marv that I'm like, it's like

334
00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:26.440
my dad.

335
00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:27.720
I'd like to spend an afternoon with him.

336
00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:29.120
He does.

337
00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:30.440
I mean, he's perfect casting.

338
00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.500
In this scene, Ned is basically just kissing up to Marv.

339
00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.320
Ken is annoyed by it.

340
00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:39.480
But then Ken joins in on kissing up to Marv.

341
00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:39.920
Yes.

342
00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:46.900
Then the next scene, Ned is in his office and he's watching Mare through the blinds.

343
00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:48.780
He's given her a card.

344
00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:50.320
It's an apology card.

345
00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:52.880
It's a photo of a basset hound.

346
00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.100
The card was originally supposed to say, sorry, I missed your birthday.

347
00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:00.560
But Ned crosses out some letters and writes some stuff in.

348
00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:05.460
So now it says, sorry, I misidentified your sexuality.

349
00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:07.280
And Marv throws it in the trash.

350
00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:11.540
Well, Jen had a little story to share about choosing this particular card.

351
00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:12.640
Let's listen to it.

352
00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:19.900
One tiny little behind the scenes moment that I just remembered today was that when Ned gave

353
00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:24.040
Mare a card that said, I'm sorry, I misidentified your sexuality.

354
00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:27.780
I remember being presented from our awesome props department,

355
00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:40.540
a bunch of basset hound cards and basset hound images to put on the card and trying to figure out which sad basset hound looked most like sad donal or sad Ned.

356
00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:42.000
And that was just delightful.

357
00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:43.840
And one of those, what are our jobs moment?

358
00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:44.620
This is amazing.

359
00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:47.780
What did you do today?

360
00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.860
I had to pick a sad basset hound card.

361
00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:51.420
Such attention to detail.

362
00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:51.960
I know.

363
00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:55.760
Well, now Mare has a talking head and she basically says, you know what?

364
00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:57.880
I don't need to be friends with everyone I work with.

365
00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.560
And then she kind of goes after the documentary crew.

366
00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:03.280
She's like, do you guys ever ask questions about journalism?

367
00:15:04.380 --> 00:15:04.600
Yeah.

368
00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:06.540
I guess they don't.

369
00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:07.600
Apparently not.

370
00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:09.660
I do have a wardrobe check for you.

371
00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:10.360
What is it?

372
00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:13.500
I love Mare's sweater so much, right?

373
00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:14.200
Yes.

374
00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:14.640
Okay.

375
00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:15.940
So I went on this website.

376
00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:18.400
It's called wornontv.net.

377
00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:19.660
W-O-R-N.

378
00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:23.300
You know, wornontv.net to see if they might have the details.

379
00:15:23.300 --> 00:15:24.260
And they did.

380
00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:24.720
What?

381
00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:25.900
Here you go.

382
00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:31.240
It is a cashmere sweater made by Marnie and they're out of the UK.

383
00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:33.520
I want you to guess what it retails for.

384
00:15:34.980 --> 00:15:35.540
$175.

385
00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.040
$1,569.

386
00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.120
Mare can't afford that.

387
00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:41.760
I know.

388
00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:43.160
And that's USA dollars.

389
00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:45.460
So I went to the website because I'm like, that can't be right.

390
00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:50.780
And I selected the US version, you know, and that's how much that sweater costs.

391
00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:53.180
The Marnie Color Block Cashmere Jumper.

392
00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:54.960
Wow.

393
00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:55.720
I know.

394
00:15:55.720 --> 00:16:02.020
And we're sure that she's not wearing like someone's knockoff version of this cashmere sweater.

395
00:16:02.260 --> 00:16:03.680
It's for sure the one.

396
00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:04.120
It's for sure.

397
00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:05.820
It's really beautiful.

398
00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:06.820
It's beautiful.

399
00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:07.600
I noticed it.

400
00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:11.240
And I, I mean, I will share in stories because I have the link to it.

401
00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:12.080
It is the sweater.

402
00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:17.620
I don't think I could wear a thousand dollar cashmere sweater and leave the house.

403
00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.240
I mean, we all know what I did with my watch that I thought was worth anything.

404
00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:23.140
You put it, you locked it up.

405
00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:24.160
A safe deposit box.

406
00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:32.600
If something's too expensive, I don't understand how people spend thousands of dollars on handbags.

407
00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:35.880
Like, how do you ever take it anywhere?

408
00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.780
That's how I feel about sunglasses.

409
00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:42.940
Like, I am going to drop them, shove them in a purse by accident.

410
00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.040
Here it is.

411
00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:45.760
Look, I have a picture of it.

412
00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.140
The website is called Farfetch.

413
00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:49.120
There it is.

414
00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:51.020
That's it?

415
00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:51.760
That's it.

416
00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:55.160
I didn't realize the one sleeve was all burgundy.

417
00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:55.640
Yeah.

418
00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:58.080
Well, yeah, I couldn't wear it.

419
00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:00.560
I'd buy the knockoff version.

420
00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:01.140
Mm-hmm.

421
00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:02.300
Probably wouldn't fit as well.

422
00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:04.200
That's how they get you.

423
00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:04.800
That's how they get you.

424
00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:08.100
So, next up, Marv is talking to Adam.

425
00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:12.020
And Adam says, Marv, look at this old newspaper article.

426
00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:14.600
There's this profile of you as a young businessman.

427
00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.960
And Marv is like, oh my gosh, where'd all my hair go?

428
00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:20.700
And Adam's like, I don't know.

429
00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:22.920
That was a real question.

430
00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:28.640
And then he says, oh, we also have an obituary ready to go for you.

431
00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:30.460
So, when you die, it's all set.

432
00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:35.120
It's like, all I could think about is how they have the Super Bowl winner's baseball hats ready.

433
00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:37.800
Oh my God, we talk about that all the time.

434
00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:38.220
Yeah.

435
00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:39.620
So, they're ready to go.

436
00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:42.720
But they don't know who's winning, but they all immediately have a baseball hat.

437
00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:49.380
It's so wasteful because there's all this merch that just gets tossed, trashed.

438
00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:54.340
I think about that every time I see them put the hats on within three seconds of winning.

439
00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:54.900
I know.

440
00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:57.760
But anyway, they have his obituary ready to go.

441
00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:01.760
I mean, he lets Marv read it, and Marv's a little bummed out by it.

442
00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:07.140
He's like, oh, maybe my life hasn't been as meaningful as I'd hoped now that I read it in black and white.

443
00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:08.820
I mean, I think it's like two paragraphs.

444
00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.420
It doesn't seem like they put a lot of effort into it.

445
00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:15.180
You know, when I worked at a local television station in college,

446
00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:19.720
we had some pre-edited obituary packages.

447
00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:20.820
Oh, yeah.

448
00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:24.260
Like, they've got the video montage ready and the whole thing.

449
00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:30.180
Especially if someone is maybe sick or, like, a very famous person is very old.

450
00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:31.600
They kind of have it ready to go.

451
00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:34.760
I remember thinking, my gosh, it's just so bleak.

452
00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:35.120
Yeah.

453
00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:37.860
But this led me on a deep dive.

454
00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:39.080
Of what?

455
00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:47.260
Did you know that there is a Wikipedia page that is a list of prematurely released obituaries?

456
00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:49.880
Of famous people?

457
00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:50.580
Yeah.

458
00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:53.660
I mean, the famous people are on the Wikipedia page.

459
00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:55.900
I don't know if it's happened to non-famous people.

460
00:18:56.020 --> 00:19:00.320
It probably has if they, for some reason, had a pre-written obituary.

461
00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:09.940
But did you know that Rowan Atkinson has falsely been reported as dead three times in 2016, 2017, and 2018?

462
00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:11.040
What the heck?

463
00:19:11.240 --> 00:19:11.640
I don't know.

464
00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:12.600
He's still alive.

465
00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:12.940
Yeah.

466
00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:16.580
And he's had to, like, say, hey, guess what?

467
00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:16.760
Yeah.

468
00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:18.480
I don't know why that's happening to him.

469
00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:32.580
Kirk Douglas had a draft of his obituary leaked on people.com in November of 2014, and it still had the headline, Do Not Publish.

470
00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:37.020
And he didn't actually die for another six years.

471
00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:40.000
He was 103 when he passed away.

472
00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:41.300
Crazy.

473
00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:42.080
Yeah.

474
00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:44.860
But then this one really got me.

475
00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:45.860
P.T.

476
00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:51.860
Barnum had a premature obituary published, but it was his requeSt.

477
00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:58.880
I guess when he took to his deathbed, he said he would sure love to read what the papers would say about him.

478
00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:03.760
So, they printed his obituary two weeks before his actual death.

479
00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:08.560
Oh, he wanted to be, like, basically privy to his own funeral.

480
00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:10.160
Well, that's the thing.

481
00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:13.680
I mean, people have sometimes said, like, why can't I be at my funeral?

482
00:20:14.220 --> 00:20:17.800
Like, can I hear all the speeches and how much people loved me?

483
00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:18.700
It's kind of a shame.

484
00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:19.360
I miss it.

485
00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:20.160
Yeah.

486
00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:22.660
So, I guess that was his way of getting that.

487
00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:27.340
But Marv was not interested in reading his premature obituary.

488
00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:28.080
It bummed him out.

489
00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:28.940
It really did.

490
00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:33.560
Now we have a little sort of runner of people reacting to Marv being in the bullpen.

491
00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:35.340
Like, Detrick freezes around him.

492
00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:39.040
Atalola says, oh, I don't, like, suck up to people.

493
00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:42.280
But then she's, like, super overly chipper and smiley.

494
00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:45.540
And then we're going to have a scene between Esmeralda and Ned.

495
00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:48.320
And she's got a stack of stories that Marv approved.

496
00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:51.520
But Ned's like, wait, I had sort of scrapped some of those.

497
00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:53.600
I have a question.

498
00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:54.040
Yeah.

499
00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:57.460
Why is Ned killing any stories?

500
00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.780
Do they have so much content that he can just kill stories?

501
00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:03.780
I feel like we're in a beggars can't be chooser situation here.

502
00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:05.000
We 100% are.

503
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:11.820
This whole episode starts with a completely empty dry erase board with the word story ideas and nothing under it.

504
00:21:11.960 --> 00:21:15.380
And I'm like, how are they putting out a newspaper every day?

505
00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:20.180
Like, I feel like they would be lucky to have a single weekend paper.

506
00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:22.020
Not a Sunday paper, too thick.

507
00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:24.200
A Saturday, once a week paper.

508
00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:25.780
A Saturday, here's what's happening around town.

509
00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:27.760
Well, here's the thing.

510
00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:30.140
Ned is going to go approach Marv about it.

511
00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:32.900
And Marv is talking to Kimberly.

512
00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:35.980
And as Ned approaches, he's like, hey, buddy, how you doing?

513
00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:37.040
You know, Kimberly here.

514
00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.800
She just sold her one millionth roll of softies.

515
00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:45.400
